2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The poem is Sidney's double sestina "Ye Goatherd Gods." All 12 six-line stanzas already obey the exact terminal-word rotation (6-1-5-2-4-3) with end-words A=mountains, B=valleys, C=forests, D=music, E=morning, F=evening; the envoi must deploy all six words across three lines in the canonical order A-B / C-D / E-F.  
Key anomalies: (a) line 25 ("These forests eke... music") produces a plural-subject/singular-verb clash ("forests... Hath") and is the only duplicated terminal word; (b) line 74 ("Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music") is a sentence fragment whose demonstrative and "Long since" phrasing belong in stanza 5; (c) line 73 is a well-formed clause whose placement at the head of stanza 5 restores grammar, reference, and the required envoi.  
Swapping 25 and 74 therefore satisfies rotation, grammar, and the attested envoi simultaneously; every other proposed pair either breaks the rotation, duplicates an end-word, or leaves the subject-verb error untouched. No other pair meets all three constraints.}

